The three variants differ in how the ground-truth SDF gradient is applied: with a distance-adaptive step size $\alpha$ (Oracle-adaptive), a fixed $\alpha = 0.005$ (Oracle-fixed), or in the model's latent feature space (Oracle-feature); see Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_oracle} for details. Per-task analysis reveals the mechanism (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}). On the one task where the EE is the primary collision body, oracle refinement helps (+9 points). On the remaining three, the SDF gradient pushes the EE away from obstacles but rotates intermediate arm links into obstacle volumes; the net effect is one collision traded for another. The A3 learned SDF never triggers refinement (0 steps per episode).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\columnwidth]{fig_oracle_bar.pdf}
\caption{CAR for oracle SDF refinement versus unrefined baselines. All GT oracle variants underperform baselines despite perfect distance information.}
\label{fig:oracle_bar}
\end{figure}
EE-only refinement is structurally counterproductive: the SDF gradient displaces the EE, but the resulting joint-angle changes rotate intermediate links through unmonitored volumes. On \texttt{orange\_juice} (LoRA CAR\,=\,96\%), Oracle-adaptive reduces CAR to 82.5\% as each step swings the elbow into the obstacle. Feature-space application yields no advantage, confirming the failure is geometric rather than representation-dependent. To rule out action-collapse as a confound, we apply Oracle-adaptive to Pi0.5 LoRA (0\% action-collapse, CAR\,=\,57.0\%): CAR drops to 51.9\% ($-$5.1\,pp), confirming that the counterproductive effect is structural and not an artifact of near-frozen baselines.

